    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, take the derivative f’(x) and set it equal to zero. Solve for x to find critical points. Then, plug those x-values back into f(x) to get the y-coordinates. For example, with f(x) = x³ - 3x, f’(x) = 3x² - 3. Setting to zero gives x = ±1, so stationary points are (1, -2) and (-1, 2).",
